# 3D metal-organic frameworks based on lanthanide-seamed dimeric pyrogallol[4]arene nanocapsules

1State Key Laboratory of Structural Chemistry, Fujian Institute of Research on the Structure of Matter, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Fuzhou 350002, China; 2University of the Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100049, China
Two novel 3D metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with cds network, $\{[\mathrm{MeNH_{3}}]_{\gamma}[\mathrm{Ln_{8}(P g C_{2})_{2}(H_{2}O)_{y}(H C O O)_{7}}]\}_{n}\cdot x(\mathrm{Solven})$ t) (FJIY4, FJI=Fujian Institute, $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Ln=Gd}}$ , $y{=}12$ ; FJI-Y5, $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Ln}}={\mathrm{Dy}}$ , $_{y=11}$ ; $\mathrm{PgC}_{2}{=}C.$ -ethylpyrogallol[4]arene), based on unprecedented dimeric pyrogallol[4]arene-based $\mathrm{Ln}_{8}$ metal-organic nanocapsule (MONC) supramolecular building blocks and formate linkers, have been prepared under solvothermal conditions. To our best of knowledge, they present not only the first two examples of 3D hierarchical structures constructed from MONCs in metal-pyrogallol[4]arene system, but also the first two examples of MOFs based on lanthanide MONCs. Remarkably, the inner cavity volume of the $\mathrm{Ln}_{8}$ capsule in FJI-Y4 and FJI-Y5 is approximately $151\mathrm{~\AA~}^{3}$ , which is larger than those found in previous transition metal-seamed dimeric $\mathrm{PgC}_{n}$ -based MONCs. Magnetic investigation on FJI-Y4 suggests a significant magnetocaloric effect $(23.97~\mathrm{J}\log^{-1}\mathrm{K}^{-1}$ , $\Delta H{=}7$ T, $2.5\mathrm{K}$ ), while FJI-Y5 exhibits slow relaxation of the magnetization.
## lanthanide, pyrogallol[4]arene, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), magnetic propeties
Citation: Su K, Wu M, Wang W, Zhou M, Yuan D, Hong M. 3D metal-organic frameworks based on lanthanide-seamed dimeric pyrogallol[4]arene nanocapsules. Sc China Chem, 2018, 61, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11426-017-9220-4
## 1 Introduction
Design and construction of metal-organic nanocapsules (MONCs) with specific geometries and cavities have attracted considerable attention [1,2], and have been applied in many frontier research including sensing [3,4], catalysis [5,6], drug deliver [7–9] and so on [10–14]. Chemists have paid much attention to preparing new MONCs by metal ions or metal clusters with different coordination environments and organic linkers with various shapes from the past two decades [15–19]. However, utilization of the MONCs in the construction of more complicated hierarchical structures still remains largely unexplored [20,21].
Pyrogallol[4]arenes $({\mathrm{Pg}}\mathrm{C}_{n}\mathrm{s})$ , the vase-shaped macrocyclic host molecules composed of four pyrogallol groups in their upper rims, have been documented to be excellent building blocks for constructing supramolecular structural motifs including supramolecular organic frameworks (SOFs) [22– 24], hydrogen-bonded/metal-organic nanotubes [25,26], hydrogen-bonded capsules [27–29], and MONCs [30,31]. Formation of dimeric and/or hexameric MONCs has been achieved by constructing $\mathrm{PgC}_{n}\mathbf{s}$ with $\mathrm{Mg^{H},C o^{I I},N i^{\prime},C u^{I I}},$ $Z\mathrm{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ and $\mathbf{Ga}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ions [32–41]. Recently, the studies in this area have focused on the following three aspects: (1) synthesis of new MONCs; (2) controlling assembly of the MONCs; (3) using these MONCs as supramolecular building blocks (SBBs) in the construction of hierarchical structures. The first reported hierarchical structure in this system is a 2D framework of copper-seamed hexameric MONCs, which was synthesized by connecting the tailed hydroxyl groups of $\mathrm{PgC}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ with adjacent hexameric MONCs via copper-hydroxyl coordination, in 2005 [42]. Very recently, the similar 2D framework of nickel-seamed hexameric MONCs has also been obtained [43]. In addition, 1D chains of copper-seamed dimeric MONCs [44] and 2D networks of zinc-seamed dimeric MONCs [45], linked by bipyridine ligands, have also been reported. However, there is still no 3D hierarchical structure based on $\mathrm{PgC}_{n}$ -based MONCs.
Lanthanides (Lns) with unique chemical properties are frequently used to prepare coordination complexes [46,47], which have potential applications in catalysis [48–50], luminescent sensing [51–53], powerful magnets [54–59], and magnetic refrigeration [60–62]. In contrast to the widely exploited transition metals (TMs), Lns are less utilized in the construction of MONCs [3,63]. In addition, 3D MOFs based on lanthanide MONCs are still not reported. Herein, we report two unprecedented 3D MOFs based on lanthanideseamed dimeric pyrogallol[4]arene nanocapsules, $\{[\mathrm{MeNH_{3}}]_{\gamma}[\mathrm{Ln_{8}(P g C_{2})_{2}(H_{2}O)_{\mathrm{y}}(H C O O)_{\mathrm{7}}}]\}_{n}\cdot x(\mathrm{Solvent)}$ (FJIY4, FJI=Fujian Institute, $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Ln}}={\mathrm{Gd}}$ , $y{=}12$ ; FJI-Y5, $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Ln}}={\mathrm{Dy}}$ , $_{y=11}$ ; $\mathrm{PgC}_{2}{=}C.$ -ethylpyrogallol[4]arene). To our knowledge, these two MOFs present not only the first two examples of lanthanide coordination compounds supported by $\mathrm{PgC}_{n}$ macrocycles, but also the first two examples of 3D MOFs based on lanthanide MONCs. In addition, magnetic studies reveal that FJI-Y4 shows significant property in magnetic entropy, and FJI-Y5 exhibits slow magnetic relaxation.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Materials and measurement
$\mathrm{PgC}_{2}$ was synthesized by a literature procedure [33], while other reagent-grade chemicals from commercial sources were employed without further purification. Elemental analyses (C, H, N) were measured on a German Elementary Varil EL III instrument. The powder $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction (PXRD) data were collected by a Rigaku Mini $600~\mathrm{X}$ -ray diffractometer using $\mathrm{{Cu}\ K a}$ radiation $(\lambda{=}0.154\mathrm{\AA})$ at room temperature. Magnetic susceptibilities were performed on a Quantum Design PPMS-9T and MPMS-XL systems in the temperature range of $2{-}300\mathrm{K}$ . All experimental magnetic data were corrected for the diamagnetism of the sample holders and of the constituent atoms according to the Pascal’s constants.
## 2.2 Syntheses ofFJI-Y4 and FJI-Y5
FJI-Y4: A mixture of $\mathrm{GdCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ( $100~\mathrm{{mg}}$ , 0.27 mmol), $\mathrm{PgC}_{2}$ $\mathrm{66~mg}$ , $0.10\mathrm{mmol},$ ) in $N_{\mathbf{\ell}}$ -methylformamide (NMF) $(2\mathrm{mL})$ and $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ $(0.5\mathrm{mL})$ was sealed in an $8~\mathrm{mL}$ glass vial at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . After cooled to room temperature, block crystals of FJI-Y4 were isolated by filtration, washed with NMF, and air dried (yield: $82\%$ based on metal). Elemental analysis: Anal. Calcd. $(\%)$ for FJI-Y4: C, 31.26; H, 3.51; N, 2.97. Found (after being exchanged by $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ and dried in vacuum): C, 33.06; H, 3.41; N, 3.12. The phase purity of the sample was determined by PXRD (Figure S1, Supporting Information online).
FJI-Y5: Equivalent synthesis procedures were carried out employing $\mathrm{DyCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(100\mathrm{mg},0.27\mathrm{mmol})$ to obtain the corresponding products. Block crystals of FJI-Y5 were isolated by filtration, washed with NMF, and air dried (yield: $78\%$ based on metal). Elemental analysis: Anal. Calcd. $(\%)$ for FJI-Y5: C, 31.03; H, 3.42; N, 2.95. Found (after being exchanged by $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ and dried in vacuum): C, 32.86; H, 3.27; N, 2.88. The phase purity of the sample was determined by PXRD (Figure S1).
## 2.3 X-ray data collection and structure determination
Intensity data of FJI-Y4 and FJI-Y5 were measured on a SuperNova diffractometer which is equipped with a copper micro-focus X-ray sources $\left(\lambda{=}1.5406\mathrm{\AA}\right)$ at 100.0(3) K. The crystal structures were resolved by direct methods and all calculations were performed on the SHELXL-2017 program package [64]. Hydrogen atoms of the organic ligands were added in the riding model and refined with isotropic thermal parameters. However, hydrogen atoms on coordinated water cannot be generated, but they were included in the molecular formula directly. Moreover, diffuse electron density together with highly disordered solvent molecules of crystallization could not be generated and were not included for both structures, thus the crystal structures were treated by the “SQUEEZE” method [65], a part of the PLATON package of crystallographic software. This had dramatically improved the agreement indices. Hydrogen atoms of the organic ligands were generated theoretically onto the specific atoms and refined isotropically with fixed thermal factors. Some hydrogen atoms on coordinated water molecules cannot be generated but they were included in the molecular formula directly. The unidentified solvent molecules and seven $\mathrm{MeNH}_{3}^{+}$ cations were not included for all the two structures. Moreover, the high $R_{1}$ and $w R_{2}$ factor for FJI-Y4 and FJI-Y5 might be due to the weak crystal diffraction, which is typical in such system. The selected crystal data and determination parameters for both MOFs are summarized in Table 1. Moreover, the crystal structures of both MOFs had been deposited at the Cambridge Crystallographic Data Centre, which gave the deposition numbers CCDC 1577233 and 1577234 for FJI-Y4 and FJI-Y5, respectively.
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Crystal structures
The solvothermal synthesis of $\mathrm{PgC}_{2}$ with $\mathrm{LnCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$
$\mathrm{{Ln}\mathrm{{=}G d}}$ and Dy) in the presence of $\mathrm{NMF/H_{2}O}$ (4:1, $\nu/\nu_{;}$ ; ${\mathrm{NMF}}{=}N.$ -methylformamide) affords MOFs FJI-Y4 and FJIY5, respectively. These two MOFs are structurally analogous, and show similarity in the coordination environment. Thus we only provide the structure of FJI-Y4 as a generic description. Single-crystal X-ray diffraction experiments reveal that FJI-Y4 crystallizes in the monoclinic space group $P2_{1}/c$ , which is constructed from dimeric $\mathrm{Gd}_{8}$ MONC SBBs and $\mathrm{HCOO}^{-}$ linkers. The overall geometry of this $\mathrm{Gd}_{8}$ MONC is similar to the previously reported dimeric $\mathrm{TM_{8}}$ MONCs ( $\scriptstyle\mathrm{{TM=Co}}$ , Ni, Cu and $Z\mathrm{n}{\dot{}}$ ) [33,44,45]. As shown in Figure 1(a), the dimeric $\mathrm{Gd}_{8}$ MONC is composed of two $\mathrm{PgC}_{2}$ ligands in a staggered style, and they are further seamed together with eight $\mathbf{G}\mathbf{d}^{\mathrm{III}}$ centers to form an equatorial ring with the diameter of $11.2\mathrm{\AA}$ . The phenoxy groups of $\mathrm{PgC}_{2}$ ligands are fully deprotonated, and each pyrogallol unit is connected to two metal ions to stabilize the equatorial $\mathrm{Gd}_{8}$ ring, with $\mathrm{Gd}^{\mathrm{III}}{\cdots}{\bf G}^{\mathrm{III}}$ distances ranging from 4.155 to $4.453\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . Oxygen donors occupy all the coordination sites of the eight metal centers (Figure S2). Except one $\mathbf{G}\mathbf{d}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ion (Gd5) is nine-coordinated in distorted mono-capped squareantiprism geometry, the rest seven $\mathbf{G}\mathbf{d}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ions are eight-coordinated in distorted square-antiprism geometries (Figure 1 (b, c)). The calculated inner cavity volume of this dimeric $\mathrm{Gd}_{8}$ capsule is approximately $151\mathrm{~\AA~}^{3}$ , which is estimated using the VOIDOO program [66]. This value is larger compared to those in dimeric $\mathrm{PgC}_{n}$ -based Co-, Ni-, Cu- and Zn-seamed MONCs with cavity volumes in the range of $139{-}142\mathrm{~}\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3}$ (Table 2). This is due to the radius of $\mathbf{G}\mathbf{d}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ion is much larger than those of 3d TMs.
Each $\mathrm{Gd}_{8}$ capsule SBB is connected with four SBBs by 10 $\mathrm{HCOO}^{-}$ anions, and these four SBBs are in two different orientations (Figure 2(a, b)), which gives rise to a 3D MOF rather than an expanded version of the 2D assembly in FJIY4. In this structure, the $\mathrm{Gd}_{8}$ capsule SBB is considered as a 4-connected node and $\operatorname{HCOO}^{-}$ were considered as linkers, it can be simplified as a 3D 4-connected MOF with $(6^{5}{\cdot}8)$ cds topology (Figure 2(c)). The charge equilibrium of FJI-Y4 is balanced by distorted $\mathrm{MeNH}_{3}^{+}$ cations laid inside the accessible void. These $\mathrm{HCOO^{-}}$ and $\mathrm{MeNH}_{3}^{+}$ ions are in situ generated from the NMF molecules in a solvothermal environment. Although the overall structures of the dimeric $\mathrm{TM_{8}}$ and $\mathrm{Gd}_{8}$ MONCs are similar; the substitution of TMs for Gd ion has a large influence on their exterior coordination sites, including numbers and orientations (Figure S3). Owing to the high coordination numbers and flexible coordination geometries of Gd ion in contrast to TMs, $\mathrm{Gd}_{8}\mathrm{MONCs}$ can be utilized as SBBs to form 3D MOF, while the dimeric $\mathbf{M}_{8}$ MONCs can only link to 1D and 2D assemblies [44,45]. In recent years, a large number of lanthanide-based MOFs, which are usually constructed from the single Ln ion or Ln clusters with different nuclearity, have been prepared [67– 69]. However, MOF constructed from the lanthanide MONCs is not seen heretofore.
## 3.2 Magnetic properties
Direct-current (dc) magnetic susceptibility data for FJI-Y4 and FJI-Y5 were performed with fresh samples between $2{-}300\mathrm{K}$ with an applied field of $1\mathrm{kOe}$ (Figure 3). At $300\mathrm{K}$ , the experimental $\chi_{\mathrm{m}}T$ products are 61.61 and $112.18~\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{K}~\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ for FJI-Y4 and FJI-Y5, respectively, which is close to the expected products of 63.04 and $113.36\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{K}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ for eight noninteracting $\mathbf{G}\mathbf{d}^{\mathrm{III}}$ (4f7, $^8\mathrm{S}_{7/2}$ , $g{=}2)$ and D ${\bf\Delta}){\bf y}^{\mathrm{III}}\left(4{\bf f}^{9},{\bf\Sigma}^{6}{\bf H}_{15/2},g{=}4/3\right)$ ions. For FJI-Y4, the $\chi_{\mathrm{m}}T$ product is almost constant from room temperature to $25\mathrm{K}$ , and then decreases quickly to $53.70\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{K}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ at $2\mathrm{K}$ . Fitting the experimental data of FJI-Y4 in the range of $2{-}300\mathrm{K}$ to the Curie-Weiss law $\scriptstyle(1/\chi_{\mathrm{m}}=T/C-\theta/C)$ affords the
Curie constants $C{=}61.50\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{mol}^{-1}\ \mathrm{K}$ , and Weiss constants $\scriptstyle\theta=-0.42\ K$ (Figure S4). The negative $\theta$ value and the trend of the $\chi_{\mathrm{m}}T$ curve indicate the $\mathrm{Gd}^{\mathrm{III}}{\cdots}{\bf G}^{\mathrm{III}}$ interactions in FJI-Y4 are antiferromagnetic. For FJI-Y5, the $\chi_{\mathrm{m}}T$ product decreases gradually from room temperature to $100\mathrm{K}$ , and then falls rapidly upon further cooling to a minimum value of $83.65~\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{K}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ at $2\mathrm{K}$ . This gradual decrease on cooling may arise from the combination of non-negligible antiferromagnetic interactions between spin carriers and the depopulation of the sub-states of the DyIII centers [70,71].
It is well known polynuclear coordination compounds containing $\mathbf{G}\mathbf{d}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ions, which is isotropic and possesses a high spin ground state and negligible magnetic anisotropy, could be possible candidates for magnetic refrigeration materials. Thus the magnetocaloric effect (MCE) of FJI-Y4 can be obtained from the Maxwell equation $\Delta S_{\mathrm{m}}(T)_{\Delta H}{=}\int[\partial M(T,H)/$ $\partial T]_{H}\mathrm{d}H$ . More specifically, the magnetic entropy change $\Delta S_{\mathrm{m}}$ is calculated from the experimentally obtained field-dependent magnetization $M(H)$ with different applied magnetic field at different temperatures (Figure S5). As shown in Figure 4, the maximum of $-\Delta S_{\mathrm{m}}$ for FJI-Y4 is $23.97~\mathrm{J}\log^{-1}\mathrm{K}^{-1}$ at $2.5\mathrm{K}$ and $\Delta H{=}7\mathrm{T}$ . However, the theoretical entropy change for the spins of eight uncoupled GdIII ions using the equation $-\Delta S_{\mathrm{m}}{=}n R\mathrm{ln}(2S{+}1)$ is $36.39\mathrm{J}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\mathrm{K}^{-1}$ . The discrepancy between the experimental data and the theoretical value may ascribe to the antiferromagnetic interactions between $\mathbf{G}\mathbf{d}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ions. In addition, this value is higher than that observed in $\mathrm{Gd}^{\mathrm{III}}{}_{4}\mathrm{Ln}^{\mathrm{III}}{}_{4}$ cluster $(19.0\mathrm{J}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\mathrm{K}^{-1}$ at $4.0\mathrm{K}$ for $\Delta H{=}7\mathrm{T}$ ) supported by supramolecular macrocyclic calix[4]arene molecule [72,73], and $\mathrm{Gd}_{6}^{\mathrm{III}}\mathrm{Co}_{8}^{\mathrm{II}}$ cluster $(14.83~\mathrm{J~kg}^{-1}~\mathrm{K}^{-1}$ at $4.0\mathrm{K}$ for $\Delta H{=}7\mathrm{T}$ ) capped by $p$ -tert-butylcalix[8]arene [74].
The dynamic magnetism of FJI-Y5 was investigated by alternating current (ac) magnetic susceptibility measurements in the temperature range $2{-}20\ \mathrm{K}$ with a zero static field and an oscillation of $3.0\mathrm{Oe}$ from 111 to ${5111\mathrm{Hz}}$ (Figure 5). Interestingly, the out-of-phase susceptibility signals are frequency dependent below $10\mathrm{K}$ . However, the absence of the maximum peaks of the out-of-phase susceptibility signals until $2\mathrm{K}$ reveal that FJI-Y5 is a possible single-molecule magnet (SMM), but one with a rather small barrier to magnetization reversal. Notably, such phenomenon is similar to the thiacalix[4]arene-supported planar $\mathrm{Dy}_{4}$ cluster and calixarene-supported octahedral $\mathrm{Dy}_{6}$ cluster showing slow magnetic relaxation behavior of single-molecule magnet nature reported by Gao’s group and Liao’s group [75,76].
## 4 Conclusions
In conclusion, two 3D MOFs constructed from $\mathrm{PgC}_{n}$ -based lanthanide MONCs have been successfully synthesized. They are not only the first two examples of $\mathrm{PgC}_{n}$ -based lanthanide coordination complexes, but also the first two examples of 3D MOFs based on lanthanide MONCs. In addition, the magnetic investigation reveals that FJI-Y4 possesses a large MCE of $23.97~\mathrm{J}\log^{-1}\mathrm{K}^{-1}$ at $2.5\mathrm{K}$ and $\Delta H=$ $7\mathrm{T},$ and FJI-Y5 exhibits slow magnetization relaxation typical for single molecule magnets. Our efforts to prepare $\mathrm{PgC}_{n}$ -based TM/Ln heterometallic MONCs, as well as other hierarchical structures based on the $\mathrm{PgC}_{n}$ -based MONCs are ongoing.
